The issue you're encountering is a classic timing problem related to how web browsers handle the `window.print()` function.

### The Problem: Asynchronous Behavior

The `window.print()` method is asynchronous. When you call it, it tells the browser to open the print dialog, but your JavaScript code continues to execute immediately without waiting for the user to interact with that dialog (i.e., to click "Print" or "Cancel").

In your original code, the `printWindow.close()` command runs fractions of a second after `printWindow.print()`. This closes the new window before the browser has even had a chance to fully render and display the print dialog for that window's content. This is why removing `printWindow.close()` makes it work—the window stays open, allowing the print dialog to appear.

### The Solution: Delaying the `close()` Call

To fix this, you need to delay the `printWindow.close()` call until *after* the printing process is complete. The best way to do this is to use the `onafterprint` event.

#### Recommended Solution: Use the `onafterprint` Event

Modern browsers provide an `onafterprint` event that fires after the print dialog has been closed. This is the most reliable way to solve the problem.

Here is how you can modify your code:

```javascript
// Open a new window
var printWindow = window.open('', '_blank');

// Write content to the new window
printWindow.document.open('text/plain');
printWindow.document.write("toto");
printWindow.document.close(); // Important to close the document stream

// Set up the event handler to close the window AFTER printing
printWindow.onafterprint = function() {
    printWindow.close();
};

// Focus and trigger the print dialog
printWindow.focus();
printWindow.print();
```

This code ensures that `printWindow.close()` is only called once the user has finished with the print dialog.

#### Alternative (Less Reliable) Solution: Use `setTimeout`

If you need to support older browsers that don't have the `onafterprint` event, you can use a `setTimeout` to introduce a small delay. This is less reliable because you are guessing how long it will take for the print dialog to appear, but it often works as a fallback.

```javascript
// ... (code to open and write to the window) ...

printWindow.focus();
printWindow.print();

// Wait for a short period (e.g., 500ms) before closing the window
setTimeout(function() {
    printWindow.close();
}, 500);
```

This approach is essentially a race condition and may fail on slower computers or under heavy load. The `onafterprint` method is strongly preferred.

***

