2.  **Closest sum overall:** This means finding the sum that minimizes the absolute difference from the budget `b` (i.e., `|sum - b|`), regardless of whether the sum is over or under the budget.

A robust answer should acknowledge this ambiguity. I will provide a solution for the first, more likely interpretation, using an efficient algorithm.

### Recommended Solution (Closest Sum Not Exceeding Budget)

While a simple nested loop (brute-force) works, a more efficient solution uses a two-pointer approach after sorting the arrays. This improves the time complexity from O(n*m) to O(n log n + m log m), which is significantly better for large arrays.

```javascript
/**
 * Finds the sum of one element from arr1 and one from arr2 that is 
 * as large as possible but not greater than the budget b.
 *
 * @param {number[]} arr1 - The first array of integers.
 * @param {number[]} arr2 - The second array of integers.
 * @param {number} b - The budget.
 * @returns {number} The sum closest to but not exceeding the budget. 
 *                   Returns -1 if no such sum can be found.
 */
function getExpense(arr1, arr2, b) {
  // Sort arrays to enable the two-pointer approach.
  arr1.sort((a, b) => a - b);
  arr2.sort((a, b) => a - b);

  let left = 0; // Pointer for the start of arr1
  let right = arr2.length - 1; // Pointer for the end of arr2
  let maxSum = -1; // To store the best sum found so far

  while (left < arr1.length && right >= 0) {
    const sum = arr1[left] + arr2[right];

    if (sum <= b) {
      // This sum is a valid candidate. Update our best sum if this one is better.
      if (sum > maxSum) {
        maxSum = sum;
      }

      // If we hit the budget exactly, we can't do better.
      if (maxSum === b) {
        return b;
      }

      // Try to get a larger sum by moving the left pointer to a larger number.
      left++;
    } else {
      // The sum is over budget. We need to decrease it by moving the right pointer
      // to a smaller number.
      right--;
    }
  }

  return maxSum;
}

