An excellent question that touches on a common architectural challenge: how to design a system that handles multiple, similar-but-distinct types in a way that is scalable and maintainable. This is a perfect scenario for applying design patterns.

### My Answer

The best design pattern for this scenario is the **Strategy pattern** combined with a **Factory** (or Registry). This approach allows you to encapsulate the logic for handling each account type into its own "strategy" class and use a factory to select the correct strategy at runtime. This design adheres to the Open/Closed Principle, meaning you can add new account types in the future without modifying the existing service logic.

Here is a step-by-step implementation in a Spring Boot context.

**1. (Optional but Recommended) Create a Common Interface**

For a clean design, it's good practice to have a common interface that all your account entities can implement. This gives your service layer a consistent type to work with.

```java
// src/main/java/com/example/model/Account.java
public interface Account {
    Integer getId();
    String getFullName();
    String getPhone();
}
```

Your `DoctorAccount` and `UserAccount` entities would then implement this interface.

**2. Define the Strategy Interface**

This interface will define the contract for fetching an account. Each account type will have its own implementation.

```java
// src/main/java/com/example/service/strategy/AccountFinderStrategy.java
import com.example.model.Account;
import com.example.model.AccountType; // We will create this enum next
import java.util.Optional;

public interface AccountFinderStrategy {
    /**
     * The type of account this strategy supports.
     */
    AccountType supports();

    /**
     * Finds an account by its ID.
     */
    Optional<Account> findById(Integer id);
}
```

**3. Create an Enum for Account Types**

Using an enum for the account types provides type safety and makes the code more readable.

```java
// src/main/java/com/example/model/AccountType.java
public enum AccountType {
    DOCTOR,
    USER
    // Add new types here in the future, e.g., ADMIN, NURSE
}
```

**4. Implement Concrete Strategies**

Create a strategy implementation for each account type. These will be Spring `@Service` or `@Component` beans that use the appropriate repository.

```java
// src/main/java/com/example/service/strategy/DoctorAccountFinderStrategy.java
@Service
public class DoctorAccountFinderStrategy implements AccountFinderStrategy {

    private final DoctorAccountRepository doctorAccountRepository;

    @Autowired
    public DoctorAccountFinderStrategy(DoctorAccountRepository doctorAccountRepository) {
        this.doctorAccountRepository = doctorAccountRepository;
    }

    @Override
    public AccountType supports() {
        return AccountType.DOCTOR;
    }

    @Override
    public Optional<Account> findById(Integer id) {
        // The cast is safe because findById returns DoctorAccount which implements Account
        return doctorAccountRepository.findById(id).map(account -> account);
    }
}

// src/main/java/com/example/service/strategy/UserAccountFinderStrategy.java
@Service
public class UserAccountFinderStrategy implements AccountFinderStrategy {

    private final UserAccountRepository userAccountRepository;

    @Autowired
    public UserAccountFinderStrategy(UserAccountRepository userAccountRepository) {
        this.userAccountRepository = userAccountRepository;
    }

    @Override
    public AccountType supports() {
        return AccountType.USER;
    }

    @Override
    public Optional<Account> findById(Integer id) {
        return userAccountRepository.findById(id).map(account -> account);
    }
}
```

**5. Create the Strategy Factory/Registry**

This component will be responsible for providing the correct strategy based on the `AccountType`. Spring's dependency injection makes this very easy to implement.

```java
// src/main/java/com/example/service/AccountStrategyFactory.java
@Component
public class AccountStrategyFactory {

    private final Map<AccountType, AccountFinderStrategy> strategyMap = new EnumMap<>(AccountType.class);

    // Spring injects all beans of type AccountFinderStrategy into the list
    @Autowired
    public AccountStrategyFactory(List<AccountFinderStrategy> strategies) {
        strategies.forEach(strategy -> strategyMap.put(strategy.supports(), strategy));
    }

    public AccountFinderStrategy getStrategy(AccountType accountType) {
        AccountFinderStrategy strategy = strategyMap.get(accountType);
        if (strategy == null) {
            throw new IllegalArgumentException("No strategy found for account type: " + accountType);
        }
        return strategy;
    }
}
```

**6. Use the Factory in Your Main Service**

Finally, your main service layer is simplified. It uses the factory to delegate the call to the appropriate strategy.

```java
// src/main/java/com/example/service/AccountService.java
@Service
public class AccountService {

    private final AccountStrategyFactory strategyFactory;

    @Autowired
    public AccountService(AccountStrategyFactory strategyFactory) {
        this.strategyFactory = strategyFactory;
    }

    public Optional<Account> getAccount(Integer id, AccountType accountType) {
        AccountFinderStrategy strategy = strategyFactory.getStrategy(accountType);
        return strategy.findById(id);
    }
}
```

This design is clean, robust, and highly extensible. To add a new `AdminAccount`, you would simply:
1.  Create the `AdminAccount` entity (implementing `Account`).
2.  Create the `AdminAccountRepository`.
3.  Add `ADMIN` to the `AccountType` enum.
4.  Create a new `AdminAccountFinderStrategy` class.
No other code needs to be changed.

